## 2. Primer with worked examples

### 2.1 Repo: a secured loan dressed as a sale

A **repurchase agreement (repo)** is a sale of securities with an agreement to buy them back later at a higher price. Economically it is a **collateralised loan**: the cash borrower hands over bonds, receives cash, and pays it back with interest.

**Worked example (from the run):** a fund finances {{rx_mv}} of Treasuries with the desk for {{rx_days}} days at {{rx_rate}} with a {{rx_h}} haircut.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Client (borrows cash)
    participant D as Desk (lends cash)
    Note over C,D: Start leg, {{rx_start}}
    C->>D: {{rx_mv}} of Treasuries (collateral)
    D->>C: {{rx_pp}} cash = 100 x (1 - {{rx_h}} haircut)
    Note over C,D: Every business day: mark collateral to market
    D-->>C: margin call when collateral < (cash + accrued interest) / (1 - haircut)
    Note over C,D: End leg, {{rx_end}}
    C->>D: {{rx_rp}} = cash + interest (repurchase price)
    D->>C: Treasuries returned
```

| Quantity | Formula | Value |
| :--- | :--- | ---: |
| Purchase price (cash lent) | `PP = MV × (1 − h)` | {{rx_pp}} |
| Initial margin (the haircut, in dollars) | `MV − PP` | {{rx_im}} |
| Repurchase price | `RP = PP × (1 + r × days / 360)` | {{rx_rp}} |
| Interest (the lender's P&L) | `RP − PP` | {{rx_int}} |
| Collateral required on day t | `(PP + accrued interest) / (1 − h)` | grows daily |

**Why a haircut?** If the client defaults, the desk sells the bonds. Prices can fall in the days that takes (the *margin period of risk*), so the desk lends less than the bonds are worth. The riskier the collateral, the bigger the haircut: 1-2% for Treasuries, 10-20% for equities in this model.

**Margin calls.** Every day the collateral is re-priced. If it falls below the required level (by more than the {{rx_mta}} minimum transfer amount), the client must post more; if it rises, the excess goes back. Over these {{rx_days}} days there were {{rx_ncalls}} calls, the largest {{rx_biggest_call}} after a 2% price drop:

![Repo lifecycle](figures/repo_lifecycle.png)

**What if the client defaults?** The desk keeps the bonds and sells them. Its loss is whatever the sale proceeds (after a further price move and a liquidation cost) fall short of the loan, less a recovery on that unsecured shortfall. `Repo.close_out` implements this, and it is the core of the counterparty-default stress in section 7.

### 2.2 Reverse repo: the same trade from the other side

Every repo has two sides. Convention in this repo: we always look from the **desk's** side.

| | Desk lends cash ("reverse repo") | Desk borrows cash ("repo") |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Desk gives | cash | its own bonds (collateral) |
| Desk receives | collateral + interest | cash, pays interest |
| Why | earn a spread financing clients | fund the desk's inventory cheaply |
| Desk is exposed when | collateral falls below the loan | collateral it delivered is worth more than the cash (the haircut it *gave*) |
| On the balance sheet | a receivable (an asset) | a payable, which can **net** against a receivable with the same counterparty and maturity |

That last row matters: a reverse repo and a repo with the **same counterparty and the same maturity date** can be shown net under accounting netting rules. The optimizer in section 5 uses this to find balance sheet that a trade-by-trade view cannot see.

A counter-intuitive consequence shows up in the stress test: the **safest** counterparty on the book, a government money-market fund, produces the largest single-name default loss ({{worst_loss}}) because the desk *over-collateralises* it. When the desk borrows cash against its bonds, the haircut it gives away is an unsecured claim.

### 2.3 Interest-rate swaps and the SOFR curve

An **interest-rate swap (IRS)** exchanges a fixed rate for a floating one on a notional that never changes hands. In USD the floating leg is **SOFR**, the overnight Treasury repo rate, compounded over each period. A corporate with floating-rate debt *pays fixed* to lock in its funding cost; a pension *receives fixed* to match long-dated liabilities.

**Step 1: build the curve.** Market quotes are par rates on spot-starting SOFR swaps (OIS). From them we need *discount factors* $DF(t)$, the value today of one dollar paid at time t. Bootstrapping solves for them one maturity at a time so that every quoted swap is worth exactly zero:

$$S_n \sum_{i=1}^{n} \tau_i \, DF(t_i) \;=\; 1 - DF(t_n)$$

The left side is the fixed leg (rate $S_n$, accrual fractions $\tau_i$); the right side is the floating leg, which with SOFR projected and discounted on the same curve collapses to $1 - DF(t_n)$. For long maturities some payment dates fall between quoted pillars, so each pillar is solved numerically (Brent's method) with log-linear interpolation of discount factors. All {{n_existing}} existing positions and every request are priced off this one curve. Every quote re-prices to within {{reprice_err}} (a full pillar table is in [ANALYSIS.md](ANALYSIS.md)).

![Funding curves](figures/funding_curve.png)

The chart shows the SOFR zero curve, the forward curve it implies (the staircase is what log-linear interpolation produces: flat forwards between pillars), the input quotes, the desk's **unsecured** funding curve (SOFR plus a term funding spread), and the **secured** repo rates for three collateral types. The gaps between those lines are the desk's business.

**Step 2: price a swap leg by leg.** Worked example: a 5-year, $100mm payer swap (desk pays fixed, receives SOFR), struck at the par rate **{{ix_par}}**.

{{irs_leg_table}}

- Floating coupons are the curve's forward rates: `F_i = (DF(t_{i-1}) / DF(t_i) − 1) / τ_i`.
- PV of the floating leg = {{ix_pvflt}}; PV of the fixed leg = {{ix_pvfix}}. They are equal, which is the definition of the par rate: `K_par = PV_float / annuity`, where the annuity `N × Σ τ_i DF(t_i)` = {{ix_ann}}.

![IRS cash flows](figures/irs_cashflows.png)

**Step 3: risk.** **DV01** is how much the swap's value changes for a one-basis-point (0.01%) rise in rates. For this swap it is **{{ix_dv01}} per bp** by central finite difference and {{ix_dv01_an}} analytically (the tests require them to agree). Bumping the *quoted* par rates by 1bp and re-bootstrapping gives a slightly different {{ix_par_dv01}} ("par DV01"). If rates rise 100bp the swap gains {{ix_up100}}, a little less than the linear {{ix_lin100}}: that difference is convexity.

A real book is hedged in total but not tenor by tenor. **Key-rate DV01s** (bump one quote at a time) show where the book's curve risk sits; here the largest are {{krd_top}}:

![Key-rate DV01](figures/key_rate_dv01.png)

The 7Y/10Y pair is the signature of a hedge that matches total DV01 but not maturity: a 10Y pension receiver hedged with a DV01-equivalent 7Y swap is flat to parallel moves and exposed to a steepening.

### 2.4 Equity total-return swaps

A **total-return swap (TRS)** lets a client own the economics of a stock without owning the stock. Each reset period:

- the client **receives** the total return, price change plus dividends: `q × (S_k − S_{k−1}) + q × D_k`,
- the client **pays** financing on the notional: `q × S_{k−1} × (SOFR_k + s) × τ_k`,
- the notional then resets to the new price.

The desk pays the total return, so it **buys the shares as a hedge** and funds them: client cash initial margin first, then equity repo in the street, then unsecured funding for whatever the street haircut leaves. Equity moves cancel between swap and hedge; what is left is the client spread minus the cost of funding the hedge.

**Worked example (from the run):** {{tx_notional}} index TRS, one year, quarterly resets, client spread {{tx_spread}}, along a simulated price path.

{{trs_table}}

The client's year nets to {{tx_client}} (market risk is theirs). The desk earns {{tx_desk}}, almost exactly the spread minus the hedge funding spread on the average notional, **every quarter, whichever way the index moved**:

![TRS lifecycle](figures/trs_lifecycle.png)

**Mark-to-market.** Between resets the swap is valued with the equity forward

$$F(t,T) = \frac{S_t - PV(\text{dividends in } (t,T])}{DF(t,T)}$$

Because notional resets to market, each *future* period is worth almost exactly

$$-\,s\,\tau_j\,q\,S_t\,\frac{DF(t_j)}{DF(t_{j-1})}$$

so a TRS with zero spread is worth zero at every reset. At inception this one is worth {{tx_mtm0}} to the desk, the present value of the spread it will collect. The tests check this identity on a hand-computable fixture.

