## 3. Why balance sheet is the scarce resource

Revenue minus funding cost looks like profit, but it is not. For every trade the library charges:

| Charge | What it pays for | How it is computed here |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Funding cost** | the cash the desk needs | secured (street repo) where collateral allows, unsecured FTP for haircut gaps and margin |
| **Balance-sheet charge** | leverage-ratio capital | rate x leverage exposure (assets + counterparty add-ons) |
| **Capital charge** | risk-weighted capital | capital ratio x hurdle rate x RWA |
| **Liquidity charge** | the HQLA buffer the trade forces the bank to hold | rate x liquidity usage (unsecured funding, run-off of secured funding) |
| **Expected counterparty cost** | expected default loss | PD x LGD x exposure-at-default |

![Economics waterfall](figures/economics_waterfall.png)

Look at the right-hand panel: the 1-year Treasury reverse repo earns {{rr10_rev_less_fund}} a year over its funding, but its **balance-sheet charge alone is {{rr10_bs}}**. Low-margin, high-volume Treasury financing is exactly the business leverage-ratio rules made expensive, which is why netting (section 5) is so valuable. The corporate swap is the opposite: tiny balance sheet, but uncollateralised, so its capital and funding (FVA/MVA-style) costs eat {{irs01_rev}} of revenue down to {{irs01_ni}}.

