## 7. Stress testing

| Scenario | What moves |
| :--- | :--- |
| SOFR +100bp | all quoted SOFR swap rates +100bp, curve re-bootstrapped; revalues swaps and fixed-rate term repos |
| Funding spread +100bp | the bank's unsecured funding spread (FTP) +100bp |
| Equity -20% | stocks and equity collateral -20%; TRS swaps and their hedges revalued |
| Collateral -15% | every non-Treasury collateral class -15% (Treasuries -3%) |
| Street haircuts x2 | lenders double the haircuts they apply to the desk's collateral (+1pt) |
| Counterparty default | the single counterparty whose default costs most, closed out after a 99% margin-period move |
| Liquidity charge doubles | cost of holding the liquidity buffer x2 |
| Combined | all of the above at once, worst default under stressed prices |

P&L is split so nothing is double counted: **mark-to-market** for rate and price moves, **1-year net income** for spread, haircut and charge changes, and **credit** for defaults (close-out) or stressed expected losses on newly uncollateralised exposure.

![Stress P&L](figures/stress_pnl.png)

{{stress_table}}

The book is hedged against markets: SOFR +100bp costs {{st_sofr_mtm}} (the residual DV01 of fixed-rate term repo financing), equity -20% almost nothing because TRS hedges offset. The real risk is **credit and liquidity**: a combined shock costs {{combined}}, of which {{st_combined_credit}} is the close-out of {{st_combined_def}} with its collateral already down 15-20%.

Which default would hurt most today?

![Default losses](figures/default_losses.png)

**Haircut stress.** When street lenders raise haircuts, the desk can borrow less against the same collateral and must fill the gap with unsecured funding, which uses the liquidity buffer. At {{hc_breach}} today's haircuts the liquidity limit ({{liq_cap}} of capacity, {{liq_usage}} used today) is breached. This is the 2008 and March-2020 mechanism in miniature.

![Haircut stress](figures/haircut_stress.png)

